From 23606a5ed11a83fa64ff9f5345e530a778c5c1e1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 23:51:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] test(serve-proof-home): isolate parser test from the
 detached default-plugin installer

test_abbreviated_token_file_flag_is_rejected_by_parser flaked (CI run 44120720139, job
134171742909, slice 5/8): its parser assertions passed, then the after-snapshot's
home.rglob saw cache/plugin-catalog.json.tmp-*.tmp as a file and FileNotFoundError'd
reading it.

Five whys:
1. The snapshot enumerated every file in the sentinel home, then read each one.
2. A temp file vanished between is_file and read_bytes: utils.atomic_write_text's
   rename of the live plugin catalog into the sentinel's cache.
3. That writer is the detached default-plugins child (spawn_install): unlike the other
   tests in this file, which stop at the config-import tripwire, this one runs main's
   real boot, whose step_install_default_plugins forks the installer.
4. subprocess.run waits for the CLI only, so the child keeps fetching the live catalog
   (network) and publishing into the sentinel after the test reads it.
5. The fixture never opted out of lazy installs, and the recovery-state check read the
   whole home instead of the files it asserts on.

Fix (test-only; no production change, no retry, no timeout):
- The parser test sets HERMES_DISABLE_LAZY_INSTALLS=1 (the existing fetch_policy
  opt-out), so no detached installer is started.
- Tripwire: assert cache/default-plugins.rerun is absent (spawn_install marks it before
  forking), so dropping the isolation fails deterministically.
- The recovery-state assertion reads exactly the pre-existing files; a missing one
  reads as None and fails (nothing is swallowed).

Proof:
- Controlled race (pytest -p plugin): after the parser subprocess returns, a thread runs
  the real atomic_write_text for cache/plugin-catalog.json, held before os.replace; the
  replace is released when the snapshot's is_file sees the temp file. Old test: 2/2
  FAIL with FileNotFoundError on plugin-catalog.json.tmp-*.tmp (the CI traceback); new
  test: 2/2 pass. Writer released and joined on both.
- Uncontrolled probe: old env leaves a live `hermes_cli.default_plugins` child that
  writes cache/plugin-catalog.json ~0.25 s after the CLI exits; with the opt-out no
  child and no late writes.
- Mutants, each fails the new test: allow_abbrev=True (PARSED_TOKEN_FILE, rc 87);
  boot rewrites processes.json; boot deletes desktop/interrupted_turns.json; removing
  the lazy-install opt-out (tripwire).
- HERMES_TEST_FILE_RETRIES=0 scripts/run_tests.sh tests/hermes_cli/test_serve_proof_home.py:
  48 passed, 3/3 runs.
---
 tests/hermes_cli/test_serve_proof_home.py | 16 +++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/tests/hermes_cli/test_serve_proof_home.py b/tests/hermes_cli/test_serve_proof_home.py
index 3d525e936cbe594a3f750e5509835e92a50f9839..5be4722dcf23f9c60a38a09ee68a7b8db499d9ae 100644
--- a/tests/hermes_cli/test_serve_proof_home.py
+++ b/tests/hermes_cli/test_serve_proof_home.py
@@ -158,15 +158,25 @@ def test_abbreviated_token_file_flag_is_rejected_by_parser(sentinel, args):
     (``hermes_cli.main`` imports config at module level, so the parser rejects it after that import;
     the tripwire here is the first parse that yields a token file, before ``cmd_dashboard``.)"""
     before = _snapshot(sentinel)
-    env = _clean_env(sentinel, 'HERMES_AGENT_TOOL_CALL', {'HERMES_DESKTOP': '1'})
+    # Unlike the config-tripwire launches above, this one runs main's real boot. Its default-plugin
+    # step would start a detached installer that fetches the live catalog and publishes it into the
+    # sentinel's cache AFTER this subprocess returns (an atomic temp + rename that outlives the test).
+    # That install is unrelated to the parser contract, so the fixture opts out of lazy installs.
+    env = _clean_env(sentinel, 'HERMES_AGENT_TOOL_CALL',
+                     {'HERMES_DESKTOP': '1', 'HERMES_DISABLE_LAZY_INSTALLS': '1'})
     result = subprocess.run([sys.executable, '-c', _PARSE_BOUNDARY, *args], env=env, cwd=ROOT,
                             capture_output=True, text=True, encoding='utf-8', timeout=30, check=False)
     assert result.returncode == 2, (result.stdout, result.stderr)
     assert 'PARSED_TOKEN_FILE=' not in result.stdout
     assert 'unrecognized arguments' in result.stderr
+    # Tripwire: spawn_install marks its request before it forks, so a detached installer is visible.
+    assert not (sentinel / 'cache' / 'default-plugins.rerun').exists()
     # main's startup seeds default home files for every command; the recovery state is untouched.
-    after = _snapshot(sentinel)
-    assert {name: after.get(name) for name in before} == before
+    # Read exactly the recovery files (a missing one reads as None and fails): enumerating the whole
+    # home would also read other writers' transient files, which the contract says nothing about.
+    after = {name: (sentinel / name).read_bytes() if (sentinel / name).exists() else None
+             for name in before}
+    assert after == before
 
 
 @pytest.mark.parametrize('args', [['dashboard', '--port', '0', '--no-open'], ['serve', '--port', '0']])
-- 
